INFOIL 1.0 -- TERMS FOR COVERED RIGHTS
=======================================================================

INFINITY OPTIMIZED INFERENCE LICENSE
INFOIL 1.0

Application of this License is defined by the applicable Adoption Notice.
The adoption notice for infinity_v3 appears at the start of its root LICENSE.

1. SCOPE AND DEFINITIONS

1.1 Adoption Notice means an express notice issued by a rights holder or
authorized representative identifying: the Licensor; the code and rights
offered under INFOIL; the applicable license version; the first covered
release or full commit identifier and effective date; and any exclusions or
separately licensed material. A proposal, roadmap, or copy of license terms without an express
adoption notice is not an Adoption Notice.

1.2 Licensor means the person or legal entity identified in the Adoption
Notice that owns, or is authorized to license, the applicable rights.
Contributor means a rights holder that intentionally supplies a contribution
under this License. Neither designation assigns ownership of another
person's work or makes a promise about rights that person has not granted.

1.3 Software means the code and accompanying material expressly designated
as covered in the Adoption Notice, together with covered contributions and
Modifications. Third-Party Material means code or other material governed
by an independent license, including inherited upstream code and separately
licensed dependencies. It is excluded from the INFOIL designation to the
extent governed by its separate license. References to Software in Sections
2 through 11 include Modifications unless stated otherwise.

1.4 Modifications means changes or additions that are derivative works of
the covered Software under applicable copyright law. Separate applications
that merely call its API do not become Modifications for that reason alone.

1.5 You means the person or legal entity exercising the rights granted here.
Acting through an employee, contractor, agent, or intermediary does not
change which entity is responsible for a deployment on its behalf.

1.6 Inference means executing a machine-learning model to produce results,
including generated text, embeddings, rankings, classifications, audio,
images, video, or other model outputs. It is not limited to text generation
or services that count or bill tokens.

1.7 Commercial Inference Services means a business offering to external
customers any of the following for direct or indirect compensation:

  (a) access to Inference through an API or other service interface;
  (b) deployment, operation, or management of model-serving endpoints or
      environments; or
  (c) material assistance in deploying or operating Inference on computing
      capacity the business supplies, controls, leases, or resells.

For paragraph (c), material assistance includes customer-specific endpoint
setup, installation or configuration of model-serving software, deployment
engineering, or ongoing operational support. Merely publishing general
documentation or supplying generic hardware support does not, by itself,
constitute material assistance.

One external customer is sufficient. The customer may supply its own models,
operate the resulting endpoint, or have exclusive access to the capacity.
Compensation includes token or request charges, GPU-hour or other computing
charges, reserved-capacity payments, subscriptions, support fees, and amounts
included in a bundled contract. Neither a public marketplace, a shared API,
nor a separate charge specifically for Inference is required. Free trials
and promotional tiers forming part of such a business are included.

An entity does not provide Commercial Inference Services merely by using
Inference for its own internal operations, or by selling an application
whose customers receive application-specific functionality without access
to a general-purpose Inference or model-deployment service. A nominal UI,
API wrapper, or intermediary does not establish this application exception
when the service in substance supplies such general-purpose access.

1.8 Restricted Provider means:

  (a) an entity operating the businesses or platforms known as Fireworks AI,
      Modal, Together AI, CoreWeave, or Nebius;
  (b) any other entity carrying on a business of Commercial Inference
      Services; or
  (c) a renamed operator, successor operator, or controlled affiliate to the
      extent it operates or supports a business described in (a) or (b).

Control means ownership of more than half the voting rights or the power
to direct an entity's management. The named providers in (a) are expressly
included; they are not an exhaustive list. Once an entity qualifies as a
Restricted Provider, the execution restrictions below also apply to its
internal, research, evaluation, and unpaid deployments.

1.9 Restricted Infrastructure means computing capacity or an execution
environment supplied, operated, managed, leased to customers, or resold by
a Restricted Provider. It includes dedicated machines, rented GPUs,
reserved clusters, virtual machines, containers, serverless execution, and
customer-specific or privately accessible endpoints. A customer account,
reseller, isolated network, or dedicated tenancy does not change that status.
Capacity remains Restricted Infrastructure when passed through an
intermediary, except as expressly permitted in Section 4.

Incidental network transit, DNS, payment processing, storage that does not
execute the Software, and unrelated external API calls do not alone mean
that the Software executes on Restricted Infrastructure.

1.10 Baseten means Baseten Labs, Inc. and entities it controls when acting
as operators of Baseten's inference platform. A change of name of the same
legal entity does not remove this designation. Acquiring Baseten does not
automatically extend it to the acquirer's other businesses or platforms.

1.11 Baseten Deployment means execution on capacity owned or leased and
operated by Baseten, or execution in a customer environment where Baseten
actually operates and manages the model-serving deployment as a Baseten
service. Buying a Baseten subscription, routing traffic through Baseten, or
using its tooling alone does not qualify a deployment operated elsewhere
by another provider or customer.

1.12 Corresponding Source means the editable source for the exact covered
Software distributed, including Modifications and the scripts and
configuration reasonably needed to build and install it. It excludes
credentials, customer data, model weights not themselves covered by this
License, separate applications, and generally available tools or system
dependencies. Third-Party Material remains subject to its own requirements.

2. RIGHTS GRANTED

2.1 By relying on the permissions in this License, You accept its conditions.
Subject to those conditions, each Contributor permits You, without a royalty,
to use, copy, modify, display, perform, and distribute the copyrightable
material it contributes, worldwide and for the duration of those rights.
These permissions include source and compiled forms. Rights terminate only
as provided in Section 9; there is no discretionary revocation right here.

2.2 Each Contributor also permits You to make, have made, use, import, sell,
and offer to sell the Software under patent claims it can license that are
necessarily practiced by its contribution, alone or in the combination to
which it was contributed. This grant does not cover claims arising only
from Your subsequent changes or other combinations.

2.3 These grants allow personal, research, educational, internal business,
and commercial application uses that comply with Sections 3 and 4. Those
uses are not general exceptions to the provider or infrastructure limits.
No permission is granted for an act prohibited by this License.

3. PROVIDER AND INFRASTRUCTURE RESTRICTIONS

Except under Section 4 or a separate license under Section 7, You must not:

  (a) execute, deploy, host, or operate the Software as a Restricted
      Provider, or on behalf of or under the direction of one;
  (b) execute, deploy, host, or operate the Software on Restricted
      Infrastructure, including for Your own private use;
  (c) use the Software to provide Commercial Inference Services; or
  (d) knowingly supply, configure, arrange, or manage a deployment of the
      Software for any operation prohibited by (a), (b), or (c).

These limits apply to production, development, testing, demonstrations,
research, and free deployments. They apply to one or many customers,
private or public endpoints, shared or dedicated capacity, and token-based
or other billing. Delivering requests through OpenRouter, a reseller, an
API gateway, or a separate legal entity does not remove the limits.

Publishing a source repository or general documentation, without knowingly
arranging a prohibited deployment, does not alone violate paragraph (d).
This section does not prohibit merely reading or inspecting source code.

4. BASETEN DEPLOYMENT EXCEPTION

4.1 Notwithstanding Section 3, Baseten, its contractors acting on its behalf,
and its customers may execute, deploy, host, and operate the Software within
a Baseten Deployment. This includes providing and consuming Commercial
Inference Services within that deployment, even when a customer is itself
a Restricted Provider. Baseten may exercise the rights in Section 2 to
develop, test, maintain, and supply the Software for that deployment on
systems it operates.

4.2 The exception includes underlying capacity Baseten leases from a
Restricted Provider when Baseten actually operates and manages the
model-serving deployment. It permits the underlying capacity supplier to
provide infrastructure and infrastructure support for that Baseten
Deployment; it does not authorize that supplier to operate a separate
model-serving offering with the Software.

4.3 This exception does not authorize execution outside the qualifying
Baseten Deployment by a Restricted Provider or on Restricted Infrastructure.
Baseten acting only as a billing intermediary, sales channel, traffic
router, or software supplier is insufficient. Copies taken out of a Baseten
Deployment remain subject to the ordinary restrictions.

4.4 Sections 5 through 11 still apply. This exception neither assigns
copyright to Baseten nor represents that Baseten is the Licensor. It grants
no rights in Third-Party Material beyond those granted by its owners.

5. REDISTRIBUTION AND SOURCE AVAILABILITY

5.1 When distributing the covered Software, including inside a binary,
container, appliance, or larger product, You must:

  (a) supply this License, the applicable Adoption Notice, and retained
      copyright, attribution, patent, and third-party license notices;
  (b) mark modified files and provide a reasonable description of changes;
  (c) offer Your covered Modifications to recipients under this same
      version of INFOIL, including its Baseten exception, without terms
      that take away the permissions granted here; and
  (d) supply Corresponding Source alongside the distribution, or a clearly
      identified download of that source at no additional charge.

If using a source download, keep it available for at least three years
after Your last distribution of the corresponding version. You may use a
patch against an identified, obtainable source version if the recipient
can obtain the complete Corresponding Source and reconstruct the version
You distributed without an additional charge.

5.2 Every recipient receives the applicable Contributors' INFOIL grants
subject to these terms. You cannot grant rights You do not control, remove
the use restrictions, or claim that a different license replaces INFOIL
for another Contributor's INFOIL-only contribution.

5.3 You need not publish private Modifications merely because You make or
run them. Remote interaction with a running service, without transfer of a
copy of covered Software, does not by itself trigger Section 5.1. The
execution restrictions in Section 3 still apply to that service.

5.4 Separate applications that communicate with the Software through its
API may retain their own licenses. Including the Software in a larger
product does not exempt the covered portions from these requirements.
You may charge for permitted distribution, support, or other permitted
services, subject to the source-access requirement and Section 3.

6. EXISTING LICENSES AND THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

6.1 This License grants rights only within each Contributor's authority.
Separately identified upstream and third-party material keeps its existing
terms. Retain and comply with those terms, including applicable Apache,
MIT, BSD, and other notices. Merely bundling that material with INFOIL code
does not relicense it or confer ownership on the Licensor.

6.2 Rights previously granted under Apache-2.0 or another license remain
available under that license. Removing old branches, tags, releases,
repositories, or downloads does not withdraw such grants. This includes
previously distributed source, even if no binary or container was released.

6.3 An act independently authorized by another applicable license does not
require permission under INFOIL. Conversely, a prior grant for older code
does not authorize use of later INFOIL-only contributions. The Adoption
Notice must distinguish the rights and material offered under INFOIL from
independent licenses and previously granted rights.

6.4 No model weights, datasets, dependencies, infrastructure services, or
outputs are licensed by this document merely because the Software uses or
produces them.

7. SEPARATE PERMISSIONS AND CONTRIBUTIONS

7.1 A separate written agreement may authorize an otherwise restricted
deployment or other terms. It must identify the covered code, permitted
